epic         V3
title        Flow State v3 production
status       active
description
    **Goal:** "production code for Flow State v3 at high quality, with good error messages and the
    correctness guarantees the prototypes proved, plus example apps and generated documentation"
    (the user, 2026-11-04). Built with skill:to-epic at the library revision in skill.

    Records: Plans @plan.FlowState, @plan.Machines, @plan.Operations, @plan.Runtime,
    @plan.Persistence, @plan.EntityDomains, @plan.React, @plan.ServerRendering, @plan.Testing,
    @plan.Inspection, @plan.Diagnostics, @plan.CommandLine, @plan.LazyFeatures, @plan.Package
    (merged in phase 2, status proposed).

    Core Specs @spec.Compile, @spec.Turn, @spec.Actors, @spec.WriteLanes, @spec.Reads,
    @spec.WriteAhead, @spec.Restore, @spec.Diagnostics, @spec.Codec, @spec.StaticCli. Deferred and
    open items: code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/revisit.md,
    code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/open-items.md.

    Locations:

    - production code `packages/flow-state-v3/src/` (created in P4);
    - Flow's own tests `packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, the fixtures they and the bench share in
      `fixtures/`, and the test tooling in `src/host/testing/`
      (code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/test-system-2026-11-15.md);
    - the prototype reference `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/`;
    - feature Specs beside the core ones in `specs/` (written when P5 starts);
    - example apps `examples/` (P6);
    - docs `apps/docs/` (P7).

    Proposal files for non-verifiable phases go in `wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/`.

    ## Stages and stage exits

    | Stage | Phases | Stage exit |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | A Settle | P0, P1, P2 | The design and the prototype agree; Plans and core Specs written from them |
    | B Core | P3, P4, P11 to P15 | The core passes its Story tests, run rules and generated Stories, and the named faults are caught |
    | C Features | P5 | Each feature's slice audited, then green on production |
    | D Examples and docs | P6, P7 | Example apps run in real Chrome and Safari; docs generated from source |
    | E Hardening and release | P8, P9, P10 | Budgets met, findings swept, released as far as the user asks |

    ## Scope

    Bounds: P0 to P10, with P11 to P15 as slice phases of stage B; UI is React and Next.js only.
    Assumptions: the prototype is the reference implementation.

    Order of authority:

    - Specs, then Plans;
    - where a Plan and the prototype disagree, the prototype wins until a Spec records it (amendment
      11);
    - features are built from the Plans so corrected.

    Specs are guides, written just in time and revised with a logged reason (amendment 13).

    Exclusions:

    - Electron;
    - TanStack DB integration;
    - tla-ts;
    - new prototyping;
    - feature Specs before P5;
    - migration functions and manual versions (revisit.md).

    Reused evidence: P0 to P2's results (stage A close-out PASS after its re-check; phase 2
    completion audit PASS-WITH-FINDINGS, fixed at `9c3fc40c`); P3 rounds 1 to 5 (suite 61/61 on
    round 14, null 0/61, falsify 25/25, `nub run check` at zero, at `bc8dffba`).

    Absorbed into the Plans and cited, not carried: D1 to D7 of the round-14 review, the required
    services list (@plan.Operations, @plan.Runtime.V2), and OD1 to OD55 through the phase 2 merge
    (the design file, `machine-design.md`, is deleted; read it in git at `20986677`).

    Agent decisions:

    - the Decision notes in log.task are agents' calls, each with its reason and how to reverse it;
    - they block no work, and P4 starts without waiting for them;
    - one the user overrules becomes rework, noted then.

    ## Constraints

    - No new prototyping; production may converge again (session, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - The core and its tests are written cleanly, with the prototype open beside them; prototype
      code never moves into the package (session, 2026-11-04; P3, P4, P11 to P15).
    - One runtime, one way to run it (the user, 2026-11-15; whole run): Flow's own tests run on its
      public test tooling (Story, Fake, the runtime cut and trace, the CLI), so both are hardened. No
      model of the core, second runtime, gate file, owed list or recorded baseline is built.
    - Production code is built from `.spec` files written with skill:to-design and skill:to-spec;
      briefs cite the Spec, reviews check code against it (the user, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Specs are guides, written just in time, revised whenever building shows them wrong, with the
      reason logged (amendment 13, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Production features are built test-first (memory, 2026-11-04; P3 to P15).
    - Test the spec, not the code: expected results come from a cited Spec rule, are written before
      the code by someone other than the builder, and never come from running the code; recorded
      output is a change detector only (memory, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - UI is React and Next.js only; Electron is out (session, 2026-11-04; whole run). TanStack DB
      integration is out (session, 2026-11-03; whole run).
    - The CLI keeps only `flow check`, `explain`, `graph`, `story` and `trace` (the user,
      2026-11-02; P4, P5).
    - Docs live in `apps/docs/`, written in the source in Effect's shape (summary, When to use,
      Details, Gotchas, named runnable examples, `@see`), generated to Markdown, with long guides
      beside the package; example apps linked from them (session, 2026-11-04; P7).
    - Example apps go in `examples/`, built with Next.js; each explains what it demos with a
      write-up and tooltips; one demos Story presentation (session, 2026-11-04; P6).
    - Code style: every brief carries AGENTS.md "Code style (v3 production)", lint-bomb's
      `tools/lint-bomb/STYLE.md` with `examples/style.ts`, `booking.ts`, `tracking.ts`, and
      `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`; the core in `src/` is plain, pure, synchronous and
      deterministic, returning work as data; Effect only in `src/host/` and the edges; doc comments
      only on exported API; `nub run check:fix`, then `nub run check` at zero errors and warnings;
      the pinned `nub` toolchain (AGENTS.md; the user, 2026-11-09; P3 to P15).
    - No plan or invariant ids in code; the Spec owns them and tests cite them (session, 2026-11-04;
      whole run).
    - Lint covers the whole package: `src`, `tests`, `fixtures` and `bench` (the user, 2026-11-09;
      whole run).
    - Ask in numbered rounds with before and after and a recommendation; never present a
      recommendation as decided; call him "the user" (AGENTS.md; memory; whole run).
    - Work serially: one lead at a time, reviews on events, a close-out per phase or area
      (amendment 3, 2026-11-08; whole run). Up to 8 deterministic processes, about 16 GB; real-browser runs and
      benchmarks alone; kill only this run's processes, by exact pid (memory, 2026-11-04; whole
      run).
    - Business keys for money (`prototypes/round-8/RESULTS.md`, 2026-11-01; P4, P5). Entities are
      optional but not up for cutting (memory, 2026-11-01; P5). No tla-ts (memory, 2026-11-03; whole
      run).

    ## Authority

    By default: read, edit within the phase's scope, run checks, repair own defects, update records.
    Also allowed, by the user's approval of this Epic:

    - commit locally to `flow-rewrite` with explicit paths; never push, never stash (the user's
      approval of this Epic is the "when I say" of AGENTS.md's "commit only when I say");
    - start agents as the loop's instructions say: serially, one building lead and, at review time,
      one reviewing or checking agent (a reviewing lead, or an independent skill:check-spec or
      skill:check-task agent), each lead with its own sidecars;
    - insert Tasks, split a phase that keeps its goal, reorder without breaking a dependency;
    - start the next phase after its completion audit passes, unless it starts with a stop;
    - write and revise Specs (skill:to-spec, skill:to-design), each change logged. Writing rulings
      or coverage records into the Plans outside a merge phase: list the change and wait for the
      user's yes (AGENTS.md). Orchestrator decides: design calls in P3 to P5 and P9, the tests'
      layout and the internal module layout, design calls inside one app's slice (P6) or one docs
      section (P7), judged by a reviewing agent, and small public API changes (a flag, a default, a
      field or member name, where an option sits); small amendments to a Task's acceptance or an accepted
      proposal that keep it correct and keep the work going (the user, 2026-11-12); process experiments from a
      process study (speed rules, brief and review shape, agent types and effort), written into instructions or
      AGENTS.md with a measure and a reverse in the process notebook, kept or dropped by the next study; authority,
      stops, the reserve, gates and Task scope stay the user's (the user, 2026-11-14); each noted for the user's
      read (amendment 9).
      User decides: public entry-point names; new concepts; large public API changes (a change to
      how app code is structured, removing a feature, anything touching many call sites); a
      guarantee change; relaxing a gate; changes to the Plans' decisions or outcomes; scope;
      the frame (skill:build-a-loop#rules, R16); which example apps, and accepting each
      app; the CLI outputs; the docs criteria; budget targets; each ship stage; more agents than the
      instructions allow. Everything else still needs the user.

    ## Limits

    No budget of iterations, time or usage, for the Epic, a phase or a Task (the user, 2026-11-10):
    the reviews and each phase's exit criteria judge progress. Failure limit (the default for each
    phase): 2 failures with the same root cause after convergence. Usage reserve: the run stops at
    75 percent weekly usage, all models (the user, 2026-11-15; was 60); at the reserve the lead finishes its step,
    commits and leaves a resume note (the user, 2026-11-14, Q3 A). Usage limits and reserves always mean
    the weekly window, never the 5-hour one; read it from the harness's usage view (in the Claude
    desktop app, its usage tool; otherwise ask the user). The user changes the reserve as planned
    work changes: a new value is an edit here and a note, with no confirmation round. R20 (b) and
    R21 at the pinned revision name budgets: this Epic has none to spend, and the reserve is its
    only usage stop.

    ## Glossary

    Library terms: skill:to-epic#terms. This project's terms:

    - Prototype runtime, round 14: `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/`, the reference.
    - The conformance suite: the old private test system (a Spec model, a port, round-14 as a second
      runtime, owed lists), deleted on 2026-11-15; its last full copy is commit `52a883e6`. Task text
      that names a `conformance/` path or a gate, mutant, oracle or owed row describes it.
    - Story: a run scripted as an array of steps, as data. Fake: a second Layer for a service a
      fixture declares, answering from its own state. Cut: what a checkpoint holds.
    - Run rule: a function from a finished run to its breaches of one Spec rule, applied to every
      Story run.
    - Planted fault: a named wrong behaviour of the runtime with the rule it breaks
      (`tests/faults.ts`); `nub run faults` plants each in a scratch copy and reports which the tests
      catch. Dead core: the same check with a `step` that changes nothing.
    - Bridge: the old hand cases rehosted in `tests/bridge/`, deleted file by file as they become
      Story tests.
    - The Pay path: the fixtures (including Pay and checkout) run end to end through the public API.
    - Safari's four requirements: host adapter requirements in
      `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/safari/RESULTS.md`.
    - Issue catalog: every issue the runtime raises, with a name, cause and next step, generated
      from the code.
    - Packed-consumer check: installing the exact published tarball in a fresh project and importing
      every public route.
    - OD, C, D, U ids: decision ids of `machine-design.md` (deleted; in git at `20986677`) and its
      convergence records.
    - Amendment N: a numbered change to the old roadmap (git `b30be69d`); each one that still
      applies has an Amendment note in log.task, which names its number and says what it changed.
skill        workflow@6b7288e
run
    To start: "run the loop in wiki/tasks/V3/epic.task". You are the orchestrator: you start,
    track and route agents; you never build.

    1. Read this file, `wk show @epic.V3 --tree`, and the records the current Task links; on a
       resume, run skill:catch-up first. If status is not active or the loop fields are missing,
       stop and tell the user.
    2. Current Task: the in_progress one, otherwise `wk next --epic V3`. It returns "No ready
       tasks." while the Epic is draft (readiness needs an active Epic) and while P4 to P15's Tasks
       are draft: set a phase's Tasks open when the phase starts.
    3. Create the session cron from cadence; without one, run its prompt at each wake (R25).
    4. Keep the machine awake for the run (R26), for example `caffeinate -i` on this Mac.
    5. Recover the real agent state: agents and processes alive, branch, last checkpoint, and
       uncommitted work with its owner.
    6. Note "Run started" or "Run resumed": revision, current Task, live agents, first action.
    7. Start or resume one building lead, set its Task in_progress, and confirm activity within a
       minute. Rules: follow skill:build-a-loop#rules, skill:policy-delegation and
       skill:policy-verification at the library revision in skill; instructions override their
       defaults. The skills root is the suite path `suites/workflow` in the library checkout
       `/Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills` (a Git repository); check the pin with
       `git -C /Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills rev-parse --short HEAD`. If it is at another
       revision, read the pinned one with
       `git -C /Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills show 55c7ab7:suites/workflow/NAME/SKILL.md`,
       or ask the user. Before changing the loop fields (run, instructions, cadence, stops), read
       skill:build-a-loop at the pinned revision; before adding or editing a Task, read
       skill:to-task (and skill:to-epic for a phase); before adding a phase, read skill:to-epic.
instructions
    **Who runs the loop**

    - Every public feature in the Plans is represented in fixtures, and the same fixtures feed both the tests and
      the bench (the user, 2026-11-11). A fixture is one folder: contracts, machine, fake server, stories. Which
      features it uses is read from its compiled machine, and a feature is not built until a fixture uses it (the
      user, 2026-11-15).
    - An agent orchestrator (Claude) until a stop (roadmap, 2026-11-07). It never builds: it starts and tracks leads,
      verifies their findings, and sends the accepted ones to the building lead in the same wake.
    - One lead at a time: the building lead, and at review time one reviewing or checking agent (a reviewing lead, or
      an independent skill:check-spec or skill:check-task agent) in the review slot. Design, Spec and audit leads
      take the building lead's slot in turn (amendment 3; the user, 2026-11-11, to preserve usage).
    - One builder carries related Tasks; a fresh one starts only when a lead
      nears its fourth context compaction. Leads work through three context compactions and are rotated out only when
      a fourth is close, meaning the context is 90 percent full after the third: finish the step, commit, leave a
      resume note. A long context alone is no reason to hand off (the user, 2026-11-14; was two and a third,
      2026-11-12).
    - Leads report once per batch or stop, in at most 10 lines, no progress messages: verdict, commits, test counts,
      and the items needing a decision. Full findings and sidecar logs go in the Task's comments or a
      review file, and the report names where (the user, 2026-11-11). Sidecars report only to their lead.
    - Source of truth: the core Specs for the core; the Plans for the rest; where a Plan and the prototype disagree,
      the prototype, until a Spec records it (amendment 11; holds until P5's feature Specs exist).
    - Preview cut first (the user, 2026-11-12, wide consult Q1 A): the core (slices 1 to 5), a minimal React binding,
      IndexedDB, `flow check` and the fixtures (including Pay and checkout) in a real browser, reached when T58 and T22
      close; streams, entities, SSR, lazy features, Stories and inspection follow as later releases, reordered, not
      cut. Amended (the user, 2026-11-15): the first slice of Stories and inspection (Story, Fake, the runtime cut,
      the text trace, `flow story`, `flow trace`, `propertyTest`) comes first, as the core's own test tooling; the
      rest of those two Plans still follows later.
    - Slices interleave (the user, 2026-11-11, replan accepted; reordered 2026-11-12, wide consult W3): R (T45, then
      T51, then T47, then T60 (the suite proves its own gates, the user 2026-11-14), then T52, then T61 (the suite
      runs itself, the user 2026-11-14; linear, to stay within 5-hour limits)), T54 (core consolidation, the user
      2026-11-12), 1 T18,
      T55 (runtime life), 2a T19 then
      T20 (restore), 3a T17 (compiler), 4 T9, the browser slice T58, 3b T57 (fragments), 5 T21, 2b T56 (release
      versions), 6 T22 then T15 then T23, 7 T25, 8 T24, 9 T27, T48, T28, 10 T49, T29, T31, then T30.
      Before T54 resumes (the user, 2026-11-15): phase P16, the test system. T54 waits on T67 (the bridge ported), and
      T18 waits on T69 and T70 (the CLI commands and generated Stories).
      A slice starts with its Spec change and its failing checks: Story tests for decided outcomes, run rules for
      what must hold on every run, compile-time tests for type promises. Then the core change, then the bench. When
      a test and the core disagree the Spec decides and the fix cites its rule; a test is never edited just to
      pass. Fixtures grow with the slices: a new feature gets a fixture or a story before it counts as built.
    - Checkpoint: a builder commit at the end of a coherent step, about every 50 minutes.

    **Agents and effort**

    - Opus leads and Haiku sidecars, Claude Code (detected 2026-11-09). Every lead (building, reviewing, auditing,
      designing) runs at medium effort. Haiku review sidecars run at high; read-only sweeps (locators, surveys,
      runners) run at medium as a timed trial, compared against the speed study's sidecar times; a lead tells its own
      sidecars which (the user, 2026-11-14; was high for all).
    - Transcript studies are stopped (the user, 2026-11-15): no agent reads agent transcripts, at a Task's close
      or on a wake; the notebook stays as the record of past studies. Formerly (kept for reverse):
      Process studies (the user, 2026-11-14): at each Task's close the orchestrator runs a transcript study and
      records it in code:wiki/plans/flow/references/process/process-notebook.md, the cross-session record of
      process experiments. Read it before changing how agents are briefed, reviewed or paced. Each study records
      what it learned about the run and proposes improvements; the orchestrator applies the ones worth trying (the
      user, 2026-11-14). A process rule reaches a running lead only at its next fresh brief: a mid-session message does 
      not change
      its habits (T61 study, 2026-11-14), so new rules are judged on fresh agents (process experiment; reverse: drop
      these two sentences).
    - Speed rules (the user, 2026-11-14, code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/agent-speed-study-2026-11-14.md):
      - batch tool calls: independent Reads, Edits and searches go in one message, never one call per turn;
      - launch all of a round's sidecars in one message, so they run in parallel; a sidecar's call returns only
        when it finishes;
      - verify once per batch of edits: `nub run check:fix` alone (never a plain `check` after it), only the touched
        test files while iterating, the whole package before each checkpoint commit;
      - resume from a resume card of at most about 5,000 characters, and read files by line range, once;
      - read-only sidecars are Explore agents, and their brief carries the rules they must follow (bounded commands,
        no writes);
      - a builder that needs a ruling states it and keeps doing independent work until the answer comes; long runs go
        in the background while it keeps working, never polled in a wait loop (process experiment, 2026-11-15, T54
        study: 8.2 min idle on a ruling, 8.7 min of wait loops in 126; reverse: drop this line).
    - Building leads plan the round up front and write every committed line; Haiku never writes committed code (the
      user, 2026-11-10). Up to eight Haiku sidecars, as many as the lead judges the work needs (the user, 2026-11-14), 
      as runners (tests, the fault check, traces), one-rule diff
      reviewers and locators (prototype code and the old suite at `52a883e6` per Invariant, as file:line lists). Also
      available, used only when the lead judges them needed (the user, 2026-11-14): wrong-build writers (the
      smallest wrong implementation, in scratch, that passes today's checks), input hunters (awkward inputs from a
      Spec), and red triage (replay a red and gather file:line evidence for the wrong side; the lead decides). A
      reviewer sidecar checks every change to the test tooling or the runtime (amendment 8).
    - A lead reads every line a sidecar wrote, re-runs each check itself, confirms a planted fault fails for the
      expected reason, and logs each result as accepted, changed or rejected in the Task's comments
      (the user, 2026-11-08).
    - Processes: up to 8 deterministic ones, about 16 GB, after checking `vm_stat`; browser runs and benchmarks alone.

    **Building**

    - Every build and review brief carries the Constraints' code-style list, the Spec it implements, the
      test-the-spec rule and the requirements-first rule (Testing, below), and tells the lead to run `wk brief 
      @task.V3.Tn` and read its phase's skills first
      (the user, 2026-11-09 and 2026-11-10). Name a skill once, on the phase.
    - Plan changes found while building go to @plan.Pending as decisions, never into the Plans; one merges only once
      building proves it right and the user says yes (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Effect prior art (the user, 2026-11-11): each module's Spec skeleton (skill:to-design) lists the Effect features
      that cover its needs and why each is used or skipped; a reviewer checks it, and what a change re-creates of
      Effect is a standing review angle. Lint bans `setTimeout`, `clearTimeout`, `Date.now`, `performance.now` and
      `Math.random` in `src/` outside the real host's adapter.
    - Adapters use typed failures, defects apart; from P4, a throw outside a defect in `src/` is a standing review
      angle (the user, 2026-11-11). Tests drive production through Story and Fake (`src/host/testing/`) and read
      it through the runtime cut and evidence; a test imports nothing else from `src/` (the user, 2026-11-15; was
      the public API plus a listed seam).

    **Testing**

    - Requirements first, as failing checks (the user, 2026-11-12; the library's policy-testing at the pinned
      revision, section "Write the requirements as failing checks first"). Each Task's first commit, before
      any code:
      - the requirements list, once, in the Task's comments: each rule with its source (Spec rule or decision),
        and the cases reality cannot show. One lead gathers it with up to eight read-only Haiku sidecars (effort
        high; the user, 2026-11-14), each on a named angle: the Specs and Plans; the code and prior art it replaces; 
        the open findings,
        gaps and log notes that name the Task; and an adversary listing how a wrong build could still pass. The
        lead merges, verifies and logs each sidecar item as accepted, changed or rejected (the user, 2026-11-12).
        The list maps every acceptance item, Approach item and gap comment of the Task to a requirement, one to
        one, and step 1's review checks that mapping first (the user, 2026-11-12);
      - each requirement written as a check that fails today, in the cheapest form that fully covers it: a type
        that makes the wrong flow fail to compile, a run rule, or a Story test for a decided outcome, each with a
        planted violation it catches.
      Then build until they pass; "done" means those checks are green. A review finding is a missed requirement:
      it lands as a failing check before its fix.
    - The kinds of test (the user, 2026-11-15): Story tests in `tests/<area>/`, run rules in `tests/rules/`
      applied to every Story run, generated Stories, compile-time tests in `tests/types/`, and pure core tests
      beside the core. `vp test` and `nub run check` are the whole check; there is no gate file.
    - Builder checklist, from the miss tally and the 2026-11-15 assumption study
      (code:wiki/plans/flow/references/process/process-notebook.md; process experiment; reverse: drop this list):
      - a red counts only if it fails on the parent code with its planted wrong side, for the Invariant it names; a
        red from an import, a signature or a constant does not count; plants go in a scratch copy, never the live tree;
      - each check covers every case its rule lists (all suspend moments, all lane phases), and each expected result
        quotes the Spec sentence it follows;
      - gathering traces every existing reader of a new or changed structure and every input variant (ids with a
        slash, several Attempts, refusable writes, a fake store that refuses);
      - an unknown is never given a default (no `null`, `0`, default owner or default root): it stays unknown or is
        refused;
      - shrink and replay a red to its minimal run before triage; a root comes from each Attempt's first break, never
        a failure's text or first line;
      - an owner is the open Task whose moves can discharge the item, taken from the run that reaches it, not the area;
      - before the first edit, read AGENTS.md "Code style" and `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`; no Spec, Task or
        step ids in production code;
      - run the whole package's tests after any commit that changes ids, keys, record shapes or report text;
      - a known bug is one inverted test (`it.fails`), titled by the Spec rule it breaks and placed in its area;
        `it.todo` hides a case that stops failing, so it is not used.
    - Pre-step check (orchestrator, process experiment, 2026-11-15): before a Task's step 1, the orchestrator checks
      its brief: every Invariant owned by or routed to the Task appears in it; every cited Spec section exists where
      cited; known contradictions between the brief and the Specs are listed on the Task for the builder.
    - Shell file writes are blocked by a hook (`tools/process/block-shell-writes.ts`, in `.claude/settings.json`);
      write files with the Write or Edit tool (process experiment, 2026-11-14).
    - At each Task's close the builder runs the generated Stories over fresh seeds (until they exist, the reload
      sweep over every Story); every new failure lands as a fix or an inverted test before review (the user,
      2026-11-14, T61's approved Approach; amended 2026-11-15).
    - Which side is wrong (the user, 2026-11-12; skill:policy-verification#scope): before a red becomes a
      requirement or a fix, the lead replays it and names the wrong side with evidence: the runtime breaks a cited
      rule, or the test, the fake or the test tooling does.
    - Routed gaps are owed checks (the user, 2026-11-12; consult A5): a gap a review routes to a later Task lands in
      the same fix round as an inverted test, or as a comment on its owner Task when no fixture can express it yet.
      A gap with no rule to cite goes through the next rule first.
    - Spec contradictions (the user, 2026-11-12; consult A6; Specs are hints): the building lead writes one concrete
      case with both results and decides a small or internal one itself, keeping the reading that loses no ending;
      a guarantee change goes to the user while work continues on that side. The Spec is edited so the losing text
      is gone, with one line in its spec log.
    - Test first, at the module boundary (the user, 2026-11-11; skill:policy-testing in `.agents/skills`): before
      code, write module integration tests through the public interface with real components, not unit tests of
      internals, and the cases that enforce each Invariant the work implements; then build until they pass.
    - Expected results are written by hand from the cited Spec rule; nothing computes them (the user, 2026-11-15;
      was an executable Spec model, T45). Strength is shown, not scored: `nub run faults` plants each named fault
      and the dead core in a scratch copy, and is run whenever `src/host/testing`, `src/host/inspect.ts` or
      `tests/rules` changes. A fault the tests miss lands as a run rule or a Story test.
    - Every brief and review of the test tooling applies skill:policy-testing#audit-a-suite as a checklist: each
      check's inputs inventoried (observed through a fake or reported by the runtime), and expected results never
      copied from round-14 or from a run of the code (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Convergence threshold 2: the second review in a row finding one root cause in one part stops case-by-case
      fixes; the lead gathers every requirement and finding and redesigns that part at once (the user, 2026-11-12,
      replacing amendment 4's 3). Otherwise fix the known bugs and go on (the user, 2026-11-10).

    **Reviews**

    - One review at each Task's close, reading the diff of fixtures, tests and core, and also on events: a Spec
      change, or a change to the test tooling or the run rules (the user, 2026-11-15; was every 90 minutes and on
      each step). The lead writes each requirement the Task claims with its evidence, and the review confirms it.
      `vp test` and `nub run check` run before every checkpoint commit. A ruling is asked as two traces of the same
      Story, not as prose. A lead hands off to a fresh one with a resume note at each large step once its context runs
      long. Loop
      amendments are batched at an area's close-out unless one fixes a wrong result (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Every review goes to one Opus lead with up to eight read-only Haiku sidecars on named angles, as many as the lead
      judges it needs (the user, 2026-11-14; was two). Review speed (the user, 2026-11-14): start the test and check
      re-runs in the background before the sidecar batch; cap each sidecar at about 25 tool calls or 5 minutes; give 
      the blind
      sidecar a pack of the cited Spec rules instead of whole Specs; sidecars return a fixed table (claim, file:line,
      repro command) so the lead writes the review in one pass.
      One of them is blind (the user, 2026-11-12): it gets only the Specs, the Plans and the code under review,
      never the Task's requirements list, its comments or earlier reviews, and answers "what must be true here for
      this to be fully correct?". The lead compares its answer with the requirements list; anything only the blind
      sidecar found is a missed requirement and lands as a failing check. Every audit or research task goes to one
      Opus lead with up to eight by need (the user, 2026-11-14);
      code quality is left to lint. A close-out review ends each phase or area.
    - Stopping rule (the user, 2026-11-12; consult A4): a finding blocks only when it is in the Task's range, has a
      check red on current code, and is a lost, doubled, made-up or wrong ending, a hang, or a broken acceptance
      item; others are routed or noted. One blind review per Task and no separate re-checks: a fix round is checked
      by the next Task's review (the user, 2026-11-14). A non-blocking finding in the test tooling is noted on its
      owner Task, not worked inside the current one (the user, 2026-11-14). A Task closes on the first round with zero 
      blocking findings. A fresh Opus agent at high effort that did not gather the requirements
      reads the expected results once per Task at step 1 (every agent-ruled expectation and at least 10 percent of
      the rest) and once at an area's close-out (the user, 2026-11-14: Opus instead of Fable, to save limits).
    - Record-edit angle (the user, 2026-11-14, Q2 A): each review lists every `wiki/` file changed since the last
      reviewed checkpoint and flags any change with no labelled `Edit (agent: …)` line (Records, below); a missing
      line is a finding the lead fixes.
    - Self-contained angle (the user, 2026-11-14): each review checks the code it reads for outside imports and
      reads, temporary strings and temporary concepts (AGENTS.md, Code style); not enforced by lint.
    - Process watch (the user, 2026-11-12): the orchestrator observes each builder and reviewer phase. After every
      review it notes in one line what the review found that the builder's own checks missed, and the likely
      cause (gathering, weak check, wrong side, context, rule gap). At each Task's close it writes a short
      retrospective in the Task's comments: what was missed, why, and the process change it proposes, marked
      proposed until the user accepts it; small fixes that keep work correct and moving it applies and notes.
    - The user's page (the user, 2026-11-12; wide consult Q5): at each slice's close the orchestrator writes one
      page, "what an app author will notice": only user-visible behaviour and names decided since the last page,
      ordered by cost to reverse. The run does not wait for it, but a second slice may not close on an unread page.
    - Each slice's close reports the type-error count of `wiki/plans/flow/references/checkout` compiled against
      `src/index.ts` instead of the stand-in; it gates nothing and must fall (wide consult Q2, orchestrator).

    **Records, usage, commands**

    - Records lead with the bottom line and hold each fact once (the user, 2026-11-11): an Epic note is 1 to 3 lines
      with one Why and one Reverse for a decision; a Task comment is a verdict line, the decisions and traps that
      still matter with their reason, and a link to its review file; sidecar logs and findings tables live only in
      the review files; no commit lists or test counts beyond the one revision
      verified. Briefs and handoffs follow the user's Writing order.
    - Labelled record edits (the user, 2026-11-14, Q1 A): every edit to a Spec, Plan or Task carries one line,
      `Edit (agent: <lead role>, @task.V3.Tn, <date>): <what changed>. Why: <reason>. Reverse: <commit>.`, in
      `wiki/specs/spec-log.md` for a Spec, a Task comment for a Task, and @plan.Pending for a Plan.
    - Compaction (the user, 2026-11-12): records are compacted with skill:compact-records when the last compaction
      note in log.task is over 24 hours old, checked by the review wake in cadence; no timer, no size limits. It skips 
      the active Task's own comments
      while its builder runs, and commits only the records it changed.
    - Usage: the run stops at the reserve in Limits and at nothing else; no budget of iterations, time or usage.
    - Commands: under 5 minutes each, with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV' --` or `watch.ts --seconds N`; stdin never
      read (`</dev/null`); files only with the Write and Edit tools (memory, 2026-11-04).
    - R43: no worktrees; the builder works in the main checkout on `flow-rewrite` (the user, 2026-11-08).
cadence
    A wake every 90 minutes checks for a Task set to review, a stalled lead and the usage reserve. Reviews run at a
    Task's close and on the events in instructions, not on the clock (the user, 2026-11-15; was a review every 90
    minutes).

    Session crons `17 0-23/3 * * *` and `47 1-23/3 * * *` (together, one wake every 90 minutes).

    Prompt: "V3 review wake. Read wiki/tasks/V3/epic.task and the last notes in its log.task. If its Limits
    section sets a usage reserve, read the weekly usage from the harness's usage view, and if the reserve is reached,
    stop the run by R21 and note it. If a Task is set to review, or a review
    event has no review covering it, start one Opus reviewing lead at medium effort with up to eight read-only Haiku 
    sidecars at
    effort high (the lead decides how many), each on a named angle, at least one of them blind to the requirements list 
    (Reviews, in instructions);
    verify its findings, send the accepted ones to the building lead in the same
    wake, and note the review. If the building lead has shown no activity for 100 minutes, resume it by message or
    replace it (R7) and note it. If the last compaction note in log.task is over 24 hours old and no review is
    running, start one Opus lead at medium effort, no sidecars, to run skill:compact-records over wiki/tasks/V3
    and the review files it cites, skipping the active Task's own comments while its builder runs; it commits only
    the records it changed and reports in at most 5 lines; note the result in one line. Otherwise write nothing."
    Process studies on the wake: added by the user 2026-11-15, then removed with all transcript work (the user,
    2026-11-15).
    Quiet rule: a wake that finds nothing writes no note and sends no message; a review already covering the newest
    checkpoint counts as that period's review (R24).

    Usage sampler (the user, 2026-11-14): session cron `7,37 * * * *`, with the prompt "V3 usage sample. Call the
    harness's usage view and append one row to wiki/plans/flow/references/usage/v3-usage-log.csv with the Edit tool:
    time_ist, weekly_all_pct, weekly_fable_pct, five_hour_pct, weekly_delta_pct since the previous row,
    pace_pct_per_hour, five_hour_projected_pct (five_hour_pct divided by the minutes since the 5-hour window started,
    times 300; blank in the window's first 30 minutes), and a note of the running V3 agents. If
    five_hour_projected_pct is over 100, hold back any new review, audit or compaction agent until the window resets
    (the building lead keeps going). If weekly usage reaches the reserve in Limits, stop the run by R21. Otherwise
    write nothing else." The CSV is committed with the orchestrator's next record commit.
stops
    - The user's decisions in the Authority section;
    - a guarantee that can't be met;
    - a guarantee change;
    - a change proposal whose judge is the user;
    - the user-judged decisions in P5, P6, P7 and P9 (their cards);
    - each ship stage in P10;
    - the real-phone run in P9, which holds only that item.
